= Ḥakham Benṣion Meir Ḥai ‘Uzziel
= Mishpaṭe ‘Uzziel 4 Ḥoshen Mishpat 6
#v(1em)
= Women Voting in Public Elections

I wrote this _teshuba_ some time ago in order to clarify the _halacha_
    for myself, without intending to publish it or make a practical ruling on
    this issue. But now, since the question has been settled, I see fit to
    publish it in order to magnify Torah.

    == I: Women's rights in the elected assembly and the governing bodies of the community and the _Yishuv_.

    A stormy controversy raged around this issue through the length and
     breadth of the land of Israel, with placards, pamphlets, and newspaper
     articles appearing on a daily basis totally forbidding the participation of
     women in elections. Some based themselves on _halacha_; some on principles
     of correct conduct and modesty; some on preserving domestic harmony within
     the family. What they all had in common was reliance on the saying
     "Anything new is forbidden by the Torah." I regret that I do not have
     access to all the material written on the subject in order to discuss it,
     but I gratefully acknowledge my friend, the distinguished Rabbi, steeped in
     Torah, Chaim Hirschensohn, who collected a comprehensive summary of the
     material in the second volume of his book _Malki Baqodesh_,#footnote[St.
     Louis, 1921, pp. 171--209.] giving me the opportunity to take in all the
     opinions that forbid it and discuss them according to my limited
     understanding.

    The actual question can be divided into two separate aspects: a) the
    active right -- to vote, and b) the passive right -- to be elected.

    With respect to the first question, we have not come across any clear
     basis for a prohibition, and it makes no logical sense to deny women this
     individual right, since in these elections we are choosing leaders and
     granting our representatives the authority to speak in our name, to arrange
     the affairs of the _Yishuv_ and to impose taxes on our property. Women,
     whether directly or indirectly, accept the authority of these
     representatives and obey their instructions and legislation. One cannot
     have it both ways, by expecting women to obey the national representatives
     while denying them the right to select them!

    And if they say that women should be excluded from the electorate since
     they do not have the intellectual capacity to choose those suitable to lead
     the community, we will reply that there will never be any shortage of men
     lacking in intellectual capacity: should they also be excluded? In any
     case, both in earlier periods and at the present day, women's intellectual
     capacity and discernment is equal to men's: they are able to conduct
     business and commerce, and manage their own affairs with complete
     competence. Would anyone suggest that a grown woman needs to have trustees
     appointed against her will? When the Rabbis said that "women's knowledge is
     light-weight" (bShabbat 33 and bQiddushin 80), it means something
     completely different. So too when they say that "women have no wisdom
     except at spinning" (bYoma 66), it was only a rhetorical device to evade
     responding to a challenge posed by a women, and in fact the Talmud itself
     describes the woman in question as "wise": "A wise woman asked R.
     Eli‘ezer..." The Rabbis explicitly comment on the verse "God built [Adam's]
     rib..." (Genesis 2:22) that "this teaches that the Holy One, Blessed be He,
     gave women extra understanding." (bNidda 48)#footnote[The Talmud plays on
     the similarity between the two roots _bana_, to build, and _bin_, to
     perceive or understand.]

    As for immodesty, where is the immodesty in voters entering the ballot
     booth one by one and submitting their vote? If this is a concern, normal
     life will be impossible: it will be forbidden to walk down the street or
     for men and women to go into a shop together; it will be forbidden to do
     business with a woman in case it leads to intimacy and on to immodesty.
     Nobody has ever suggested such a thing!

    And as for family harmony, the distinguished rabbi puts it well: in that
     case we would have to deny the right to vote from children who are still
     living with their parents: in all cases where the Sages were concerned for
     ill-feeling, they made the comparison between wives and adult children
     living with their parents (bBaba Meṣi‘a 12). The objection may be raised
     that this is indeed an issue, and further similar cases should not be added
     to those that already exist. But in fact there is no cause for concern of
     ill-feeling caused by members of the family holding different opinions.
     Nobody can suppress their own world-view and opinions, and family love
     based on joint activity is sufficiently strong that it will not be
     damaged by such differences of opinion.

    Rabbi Doctor Ritter#footnote[Dov Aryeh Ritter (1855--1936), Chief Rabbi of
     Rotterdam, in the periodical _Maḥziqe Hadat_, Jerusalem 4 December 1919,
     quoted in _Malki Baqodesh_ _op. cit._, p. 197.] makes a novel argument
     against women voting: they are not a "community" or a "congregation", and
     were not included in the census of the children of Israel or their
     genealogies [in the book of Numbers] (I do not have the text of his article
     before me at present, but this is what I understand from what [R.
     Hirschensohn] quotes). Assuming for the sake of argument that they are not
     a community or a congregation or a family or a head count or anything
     whatever, are they not rational beings created in God's image? Do they have
     no common interests with the elected assembly or a committee appointed by
     it, whose rulings they will follow in all matters connected to their
     property and their children's education?

    Therefore, since we have found no indication of this prohibition, we see
     no justification for opposing women's right to vote or for dismissing the
     question, when a section of the community is in favour of it. Perhaps this
     is similar to the case where the Mishna said "Even if 99 say [that the
     fruits] should be distributed and one says that they should be left in the
     field, one listens to the latter, since they spoke according to _halacha_
     (mPe’a 4:2). And further than that, they permitted women to lay hands on
     sacrifices in order to grant satisfaction to women (bḤagiga 16), even
     though it appears to be forbidden. _A fortiori_ in the question before us,
     where there is no question of infringing a prohibition, and when forbidding
     their participation will be insulting and offensive, it is clear that they
     should be given the right to participate.

    == 2: Women being elected

    The second question is whether a woman can be elected. Now here there
    seems in theory to be an explicit prohibition (_Sifre Devarim_ on
    Deuteronomy 17:15): "'You shall place a king over yourselves'. If he dies,
    appoint another in his place. 'A king', and not a queen". From this
    Maimonides derives the law: "A woman may not be appointed to the monarchy,
    since the Torah says 'a king' -- and not a queen -- and similarly all
    leadership roles in Israel may only be filled by men and not by
    women."#footnote[MT _Hilkhot Melakhim_ 1:5.] This is the source for not
    appointing women to positions of authority, and conforms to the hidden
    _halacha_ discovered by Rabbi Shelomo Aharon Wertenheimer:#footnote[See
    _Malki Baqodesh_, _loc. cit._]
